<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  GLOBAL SCOPE, FOR ONE EVENT, AND THE REASON IS A DEFECT THAT LOOKED LIKE A DESIGN.

  `sales_order_place_after` lived in `etc/frontend/events.xml` alongside the
  add-to-cart capture observer. It read perfectly: both concern a shopper's own
  session, and an admin-created order has no search marker, so frontend scope looked
  like precision rather than a bug.

  **Luma's checkout does not place orders in the frontend area.** The one-page
  checkout submits to `POST /rest/<store>/V1/guest-carts/{id}/payment-information`,
  which Magento executes in **`webapi_rest`** — so a frontend-scoped observer is
  never registered and never runs. Measured, not reasoned: after a real guest
  checkout the session still carried
  `checkout → nitrosearch_attribution → {ids: {8: true}, q: "yoga bag"}`, and
  `queueReport()` ends by unsetting exactly that key. The marker surviving the order
  IS the proof the observer never fired. `nitrosearch_order_report` had zero rows
  across three orders placed on this store since the module was built.

  WHY GLOBAL RATHER THAN `frontend` + `webapi_rest` + `graphql`. Enumerating the
  areas a shopper's order can be placed in is the same shape as every other list this
  project has been bitten by: it is correct for the checkouts we happened to test and
  silently wrong for the next one — a headless GraphQL storefront, a third-party
  one-page checkout, a payment provider that finalises out of band. **The marker is
  the real gate**, not the area: an order placed anywhere without one queues nothing,
  which is precisely what an admin order, a CLI order and a re-order all are.

  The cost of global scope is that this observer is now constructed in areas with no
  session at all. That is handled where it belongs — `OrderAttribution` takes the
  checkout session as a PROXY (`etc/di.xml`), so nothing is constructed until a
  marker is actually read, and `orderPlaced()` swallows whatever that read throws.
  An attribution is worth nothing next to a completed checkout.
-->
<config xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
        xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="urn:magento:framework:Event/etc/events.xsd">
    <!--
      AFTER THE SAVE, NOT AFTER `place()`, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS AN ORDER ID.

      This observed `sales_order_place_after` first. That event is dispatched from
      `Order::place()`, which `OrderManagement::place()` calls IMMEDIATELY BEFORE
      `orderRepository->save($order)` — so the order has no entity id yet, and
      `(int) $order->getEntityId()` is **0**. The first real attributed order proved
      it: `nitrosearch_order_report` held `order_id = 0` for an order whose entity id
      was 4.

      Two consequences, both silent and both severe. Locally, `order_id` is UNIQUE and
      the write is `insertOnDuplicate`, so the SECOND attributed order would overwrite
      the first rather than joining it. On the wire, `order_ref` is
      `sha256(install_id | order | order_id)` — a CONSTANT when the id is always 0 —
      so the service's own dedupe would fold every order a store ever attributed into
      one. A merchant would see exactly one attributed order, forever, and nothing
      would error.

      `sales_order_save_after` fires with the row written and the id assigned, inside
      the same transaction as the order, so a rolled-back checkout takes the report
      with it. It fires on every later save too — an invoice, a status change, an
      admin edit — and that is harmless by construction: the marker is cleared when
      the report is queued, and no marker means no report.

      Still not the success page: a shopper who closes the tab has still bought.
    -->
    <event name="sales_order_save_after">
        <observer name="nitrosearch_report_placed_order"
                  instance="NitroSearch\Search\Observer\ReportPlacedOrder"/>
    </event>
</config>
